window.docContents[37].resolve({"/#linked-lists":{"contents":"Lean's standard library includes a canonical linked list datatype, called , and special syntax that makes it more convenient to use.\nLists are written in square brackets.\nFor instance, a list that contains the prime numbers less than 10 can be written:\n\nBehind the scenes,  is an inductive datatype, defined like this:The actual definition in the standard library is slightly different, because it uses features that have not yet been presented, but it is substantially similar.\nThis definition says that  takes a single type as its argument, just as  did.\nThis type is the type of the entries stored in the list.\nAccording to the constructors, a  can be built with either  or .\nThe constructor  represents empty lists and the constructor  is used for non-empty lists.\nThe first argument to  is the head of the list, and the second argument is its tail.\nA list that contains n entries contains n  constructors, the last of which has  as its tail.\n\nThe  example can be written more explicitly by using 's constructors directly:These two definitions are completely equivalent, but  is much easier to read than .\n\nFunctions that consume s can be defined in much the same way as functions that consume s.\nIndeed, one way to think of a linked list is as a  that has an extra data field dangling off each  constructor.\nFrom this point of view, computing the length of a list is the process of replacing each  with a  and the final  with a .\nJust as  took the type of the fields of the point as an argument,  takes the type of the list's entries.\nFor example, if the list contains strings, then the first argument is : .\nIt should compute like this:\n\nThe definition of  is both polymorphic (because it takes the list entry type as an argument) and recursive (because it refers to itself).\nGenerally, functions follow the shape of the data: recursive datatypes lead to recursive functions, and polymorphic datatypes lead to polymorphic functions.\n\nNames such as xs and ys are conventionally used to stand for lists of unknown values.\nThe s in the name indicates that they are plural, so they are pronounced “exes” and “whys” rather than “x s” and “y s”.\n\nTo make it easier to read functions on lists, the bracket notation  can be used to pattern-match against , and an infix  can be used in place of :\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Getting to Know Lean\u0009Polymorphism","header":"1.6.1. Linked Lists","id":"/#linked-lists"}});